{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVPC2UcvFp3axTasy1jt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UieLK787JwaJ3b8BKuFc6F","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 50,58p paper.tex; sed -n 80,96p paper.tex; sed -n 305,322p paper.tex; python3 - <<'E'\nimport json\nc=json.load(open('claims_grounding.json'))\nfor i,x in enumerate(c['claims']): print(i,x['claim'][:150],x['source_ids'],len(x['citation_keys']))\nE"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2337,"cache_read_input_tokens":41454,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2337,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"84f4710a-b57d-47ca-82ad-cb6cbbf8aa55","uuid":"faec3eb8-624b-4fdb-bc3c-56739b8532f9","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:57:32.404Z","request_id":"req_011CgVPBzSqRNtaJ5AAnvnMV"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UieLK787JwaJ3b8BKuFc6F","type":"tool_result","content":"Two lines of concern motivate this paper.\nFirst, data are noisy and finite, so a small difference between the top two competitors may not be reliable \\citep{Gao2021UncertaintyQI}.\nSecond, crowd-sourced leaderboards can be influenced deliberately, for instance through vote rigging \\citep{Min2025Rigging} or through differences in data access and sampling between providers \\citep{Singh2025Illusion}.\nBoth concerns lead to the same operational question: \\emph{how many comparison rows have to be edited before the top-ranked competitor is no longer ranked first?}\n\nWe study this question for a deliberately narrow, fully specified setting.\nThe leaderboard is a ridge-penalised BT fit; the target is the competitor ranked first on the unedited data; an edit either drops a row or reverses its outcome; and the edit budget is between 0.1\\% and 2\\% of the rows.\nA trusted evaluator, which never uses any quantity reported by the proposer, refits the model on the edited data and records whether rank 1 was displaced or, if not, what fraction of the top-versus-runner-up gap was closed.\nThe edits themselves are proposed by a one-shot first-order influence search in the spirit of influence functions \\citep{Koh2017Understanding} and approximate-robustness analysis of ``dropping a little data'' \\citep{Broderick2020Automatic}.\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Bradley--Terry models and their estimation.}\nThe BT model \\citep{Bradley1952Rank} assigns each competitor a latent score and models the win probability of a pair as a logistic function of the score difference. Extensions handle ties \\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, order effects \\citep{Davidson1977OnET}, covariates \\citep{Yan2025InferenceIA}, temporal drift \\citep{Tian2023ASA}, continuous outcomes \\citep{Stern2011ModeratedPC,Fageot2023GeneralizedBM} and Bayesian priors \\citep{Leonard1977AnAB}. Sports results are commonly rated with Elo-style systems that are closely related to BT-type likelihoods; recent work generalises Elo to the margin of victory \\citep{Szczecinski2020GElo} and proposes a score-driven rating system for sports \\citep{Holy2026ScoreDriven}. We fit a static BT model to NBA game outcomes only as a sports instance of pairwise-comparison data, and do not evaluate any Elo-style updating rule. The model has also been used to derive priorities from comparison matrices \\citep{Genest1999DerivingPF} and has been related to PageRank \\citep{Selby2024PageRankAT}. Maximum likelihood estimation requires conditions on the comparison graph \\citep{Butler2004TheEO,Bong2021GeneralizedRF}, which motivates penalised fits \\citep{Varin2024TractableRR}; our evaluator uses a ridge penalty and counts a tie as half a win for each side, a convention in the spirit of tie-extended models. Algorithms for fitting generalised BT models have been analysed by \\citet{Vojnovic2019ConvergenceRO}, and the large-sample behaviour of the estimator when the number of competitors grows by \\citet{Simons1999AsymptoticsWT} and \\citet{Li2021ThePO}.\n\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty of rankings.}\nStatistical work quantifies how sampling noise affects BT scores and ranks, through confidence regions for the score vector \\citep{Gao2021UncertaintyQI}, hypothesis tests \\citep{Makur2024MinimaxHT} and estimators of partial rankings from sparse, noisy comparisons \\citep{Morel-Balbi2025EstimationOP}. These analyses describe average-case sampling variation under a generative model. We instead ask a worst-case, data-conditional question about a single observed dataset: whether a small number of specific rows can change the top rank. The two views are complementary, and our measurements do not substitute for either confidence intervals or noise calibration.\n\n\\paragraph{Influence and robustness to dropping data.}\nInfluence functions estimate the effect of removing or perturbing a training point without refitting \\citep{Koh2017Understanding}. \\citet{Broderick2020Automatic} propose a finite-sample robustness metric asking whether dropping a small fraction of the data can change a conclusion, and use first-order influence approximations to find the data to drop. Our search is of this family, specialised to the top-versus-runner-up score gap of a BT fit and extended to outcome reversal. The linear approximation behind such analyses is the infinitesimal jackknife, which \\citet{Giordano2018Swiss} develop as a general approximation to refits with data removed; its accuracy is a property of the problem and of the size of the perturbation, and is not guaranteed for the 2\\% edits studied here. As in that literature, a first-order search finds candidate edit sets only; we therefore let the evaluator verify every edit by an exact refit.\n\n\\paragraph{Data poisoning and label-flip attacks.}\nChoosing a small set of training points to manipulate so as to change a fitted model is the subject of data-poisoning research; influence-based reasoning has also been used on the defensive side, against poisoning in online learning \\citep{Seetharaman2021Influence}. For preference data, \\citet{Yang2026Preference} present a preference-poisoning attack on offline reinforcement learning from human feedback. We know these two works only from their titles and describe them no further. Our reversal mode is a label-flip edit and our drop mode a deletion, and the first-order scores in Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects} are likewise additive per row. The setting differs: the model is a Bradley--Terry leaderboard rather than a preference-optimised language model, the objective is to dislodge one rank rather than to steer parameters towards a target, and an evaluator refits the exact model for verification. We cite this literature for positioning only and do not compare against any poisoning method, and we have not surveyed attacks on rank aggregation exhaustively.\n\n\\paragraph{Integrity of crowd-sourced leaderboards.}\n\\citet{Chiang2024Chatbot} describe the Chatbot Arena platform, in which users vote between two anonymous model responses and the votes are aggregated into rankings. \\citet{Min2025Rigging} study strategies by which votes on such platforms can be manipulated to change rankings, and \\citet{Singh2025Illusion} analyse evaluation practices on the same platform, including private testing and differences in data access and sampling across providers. \\citet{Zheng2023Judging} release expert human and GPT-4 pairwise judgments on MT-Bench. We use the Arena and MT-Bench comparison data, but our attack is a generic, evaluator-verified probe; we do not model any platform's actual voting interface or an adversary's access to it.\n\n\\section{Problem Setting and Method}\n\\item For every row compute $p_i$, $\\tilde y_i$ and $s_i=v_{a_i}-v_{b_i}$, and the effect $\\Delta_i$ of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects} for the requested mode; set $\\Delta_i=+\\infty$ for tie rows in flip mode.\n\\item Sort rows by $(\\Delta_i,\\,i)$, keep the first $k$, and drop those with $\\Delta_i\\ge0$ or non-finite $\\Delta_i$. Return the indices.\n\\end{enumerate}\nThe frozen configuration is identical to the starter code of the task (same source tree hash), so the studied method and the evaluator's influence baseline use the same first-order formulas, although the implementations differ in the details noted in the main text. % @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#_propose]]\nIts seed state is reset on request but is never used to make choices.\n\n\\subsection{Selection Context and Unscored Search Variants}\n\\label{app:selection}\nThe configuration studied here was chosen on a separate search data collection, on which it obtained a search-scope score of $0.914$ with a score delta of $0.0$ relative to the baseline (which is the selected configuration); the study outcome is recorded as completed without a qualifying winner and the selection kind is the baseline. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.baseline_score]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.investigation_outcome]]\nThree search variants were proposed as potential improvements: a calibrated minimal-perturbation sensitivity analysis across designs, a pair-class aggregated multi-rival greedy search with exact-refit selection, and rival-specific chunked re-linearised edit plans with an exact-refit prefix line search. All three produced no score ($3$ total, $0$ scored, $3$ unscored). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]\nUnscored variants are missing performance evidence, not measured losses, and no comparison involving them appears anywhere in this paper. The search-scope score $0.914$ and the final score $0.606$ come from different data collections and scopes, with different designs (the search collection has no expert-human or LLM-judge data); they must not be read as a decline or a generalisation gap.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\n\\label{app:llm}\nLarge language models were used throughout this work. An LLM-based research agent designed the three alternative search variants described above and wrote their code, none of which was scored; the studied search is the task's starter code. An LLM agent retrieved references through a literature-search API, planned the paper, wrote this manuscript and its claim-to-evidence mapping from the recorded evaluation artifacts, and produced the figures. The evaluation protocol, data, evaluator and scoring were fixed by the task specification before the run, and every number in the paper is read from the evaluator's recorded outputs. The starter references named in the task were taken from the task description.\n\n\\end{document}\n0 Final evaluation qualified and completed; qualification is an integrity gate, not superiority. ['final.qualification.outcome', 'final.qualification.status', 'final.qualification.correct', 'study.final.outcome', 'study.final.status'] 0\n1 Family-averaged edit success of the first-order influence search is 0.606. ['final.metric.combined_score', 'final.metric.score_influence'] 0\n2 Per-design edit success: crowd 1.000, sports 0.833, expert human 0.374, LLM judge 0.216. ['final.metric.score_crowd', 'final.metric.score_sports', 'final.metric.score_expert_human', 'final.metric.score_llm_judge'] 0\n3 Top-1 displacement at 2%: crowd 1/1 (drop/flip), sports 1/1, expert human 0/1, LLM judge 0/0; ordering by number of displaced cells 2,2,1,0 of 2. ['final.metric.flip_rate_crowd_drop', 'final.metric.flip_rate_crowd_flip', 'final.metric.flip_rate_sports_drop', 'final.metric.flip_rate_sports_flip', 'final.metric.flip_rate_expert_human_drop', 'final.metric.flip_rate_expert_human_flip', 'final.metric.flip_rate_llm_judge_drop', 'final.metric.flip_rate_llm_judge_flip'] 0\n4 A crowd score of 1.000 implies displacement at every budget; sports 0.833 <1 implies some smaller budget did not displace; LLM-judge score reflects pa ['final.metric.score_crowd', 'final.metric.score_sports', 'final.metric.score_llm_judge', 'final.metric.flip_rate_llm_judge_drop', 'final.metric.flip_rate_llm_judge_flip'] 0\n5 Constant and random reference policies score 0.071 each (family-averaged); win-rate heuristic scores 0.574; difference to influence search 0.032 witho ['final.metric.score_constant', 'final.metric.score_random', 'final.metric.score_winrate', 'final.metric.score_influence'] 0\n6 Margin versus best reference policy is 0.0 because the evaluator's influence baseline equals the studied search (both 0.606). ['final.metric.margin_vs_best_baseline', 'final.metric.score_influence', 'final.metric.combined_score'] 0\n7 Valid proposal rate 1.0 and permuted-input control gap 0.0 (one permuted control run per case; no general invariance claim). ['final.metric.valid_proposal_rate', 'final.metric.perm_control_abs_gap'] 0\n8 Three evaluation seeds yield identical scores (0.6057); no confidence interval available; diagnostics unverified. ['final.evidence.episodes', 'final.metric.combined_score'] 0\n9 Selection context: frozen selection is the baseline, no qualifying winner, search-scope score 0.914 with delta 0.0; 3 attempted variants, 0 scored, 3  ['study.selection_kind', 'study.investigation_outcome', 'study.baseline_score', 'study.search_score', 'study.search_score_delta', 'study.attempts.total', 'study.attempts.scored', 'study.attempts.unscored', 'result.total_ideas'] 0\n10 Appendix metric table lists all retained final metrics. ['final.metric.combined_score', 'final.metric.score_crowd', 'final.metric.score_sports', 'final.metric.score_expert_human', 'final.metric.score_llm_judge', 'final.metric.score_constant', 'final.metric.score_random', 'final.metric.score_winrate', 'final.metric.score_influence', 'final.metric.margin_vs_best_baseline', 'final.metric.perm_control_abs_gap', 'final.metric.valid_proposal_rate', 'final.metric.flip_rate_crowd_drop', 'final.metric.flip_rate_crowd_flip', 'final.metric.flip_rate_sports_drop', 'final.metric.flip_rate_sports_flip', 'final.metric.flip_rate_expert_human_drop', 'final.metric.flip_rate_expert_human_flip', 'final.metric.flip_rate_llm_judge_drop', 'final.metric.flip_rate_llm_judge_flip'] 0\n11 Background: BT model, ties, order effects, penalised fits, existence of MLE, uncertainty quantification, and algorithms (qualitative related work). ['ref.starter.Bradley1952Rank'] 19\n12 Related work on infinitesimal-jackknife approximation, poisoning defence and preference-poisoning attacks (described by title only), and Elo-style spo ['ref.openalex.W2896388162', 'ref.openalex.W7160306608', 'ref.openalex.W4287201019', 'ref.openalex.W3211957373', 'ref.openalex.W7153982762'] 5\n13 Related work on influence functions, robustness to dropping data, vote rigging, leaderboard practices and the Arena and MT-Bench data. ['ref.starter.Koh2017Understanding', 'ref.starter.Broderick2020Automatic', 'ref.starter.Min2025Rigging', 'ref.starter.Singh2025Illusion', 'ref.starter.Chiang2024Chatbot', 'ref.starter.Zheng2023Judging'] 6","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"84f4710a-b57d-47ca-82ad-cb6cbbf8aa55","uuid":"92b6b90d-a67e-4512-bd77-4904f53817d6","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:57:32.487Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Two lines of concern motivate this paper.\nFirst, data are noisy and finite, so a small difference between the top two competitors may not be reliable \\citep{Gao2021UncertaintyQI}.\nSecond, crowd-sourced leaderboards can be influenced deliberately, for instance through vote rigging \\citep{Min2025Rigging} or through differences in data access and sampling between providers \\citep{Singh2025Illusion}.\nBoth concerns lead to the same operational question: \\emph{how many comparison rows have to be edited before the top-ranked competitor is no longer ranked first?}\n\nWe study this question for a deliberately narrow, fully specified setting.\nThe leaderboard is a ridge-penalised BT fit; the target is the competitor ranked first on the unedited data; an edit either drops a row or reverses its outcome; and the edit budget is between 0.1\\% and 2\\% of the rows.\nA trusted evaluator, which never uses any quantity reported by the proposer, refits the model on the edited data and records whether rank 1 was displaced or, if not, what fraction of the top-versus-runner-up gap was closed.\nThe edits themselves are proposed by a one-shot first-order influence search in the spirit of influence functions \\citep{Koh2017Understanding} and approximate-robustness analysis of ``dropping a little data'' \\citep{Broderick2020Automatic}.\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Bradley--Terry models and their estimation.}\nThe BT model \\citep{Bradley1952Rank} assigns each competitor a latent score and models the win probability of a pair as a logistic function of the score difference. Extensions handle ties \\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, order effects \\citep{Davidson1977OnET}, covariates \\citep{Yan2025InferenceIA}, temporal drift \\citep{Tian2023ASA}, continuous outcomes \\citep{Stern2011ModeratedPC,Fageot2023GeneralizedBM} and Bayesian priors \\citep{Leonard1977AnAB}. Sports results are commonly rated with Elo-style systems that are closely related to BT-type likelihoods; recent work generalises Elo to the margin of victory \\citep{Szczecinski2020GElo} and proposes a score-driven rating system for sports \\citep{Holy2026ScoreDriven}. We fit a static BT model to NBA game outcomes only as a sports instance of pairwise-comparison data, and do not evaluate any Elo-style updating rule. The model has also been used to derive priorities from comparison matrices \\citep{Genest1999DerivingPF} and has been related to PageRank \\citep{Selby2024PageRankAT}. Maximum likelihood estimation requires conditions on the comparison graph \\citep{Butler2004TheEO,Bong2021GeneralizedRF}, which motivates penalised fits \\citep{Varin2024TractableRR}; our evaluator uses a ridge penalty and counts a tie as half a win for each side, a convention in the spirit of tie-extended models. Algorithms for fitting generalised BT models have been analysed by \\citet{Vojnovic2019ConvergenceRO}, and the large-sample behaviour of the estimator when the number of competitors grows by \\citet{Simons1999AsymptoticsWT} and \\citet{Li2021ThePO}.\n\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty of rankings.}\nStatistical work quantifies how sampling noise affects BT scores and ranks, through confidence regions for the score vector \\citep{Gao2021UncertaintyQI}, hypothesis tests \\citep{Makur2024MinimaxHT} and estimators of partial rankings from sparse, noisy comparisons \\citep{Morel-Balbi2025EstimationOP}. These analyses describe average-case sampling variation under a generative model. We instead ask a worst-case, data-conditional question about a single observed dataset: whether a small number of specific rows can change the top rank. The two views are complementary, and our measurements do not substitute for either confidence intervals or noise calibration.\n\n\\paragraph{Influence and robustness to dropping data.}\nInfluence functions estimate the effect of removing or perturbing a training point without refitting \\citep{Koh2017Understanding}. \\citet{Broderick2020Automatic} propose a finite-sample robustness metric asking whether dropping a small fraction of the data can change a conclusion, and use first-order influence approximations to find the data to drop. Our search is of this family, specialised to the top-versus-runner-up score gap of a BT fit and extended to outcome reversal. The linear approximation behind such analyses is the infinitesimal jackknife, which \\citet{Giordano2018Swiss} develop as a general approximation to refits with data removed; its accuracy is a property of the problem and of the size of the perturbation, and is not guaranteed for the 2\\% edits studied here. As in that literature, a first-order search finds candidate edit sets only; we therefore let the evaluator verify every edit by an exact refit.\n\n\\paragraph{Data poisoning and label-flip attacks.}\nChoosing a small set of training points to manipulate so as to change a fitted model is the subject of data-poisoning research; influence-based reasoning has also been used on the defensive side, against poisoning in online learning \\citep{Seetharaman2021Influence}. For preference data, \\citet{Yang2026Preference} present a preference-poisoning attack on offline reinforcement learning from human feedback. We know these two works only from their titles and describe them no further. Our reversal mode is a label-flip edit and our drop mode a deletion, and the first-order scores in Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects} are likewise additive per row. The setting differs: the model is a Bradley--Terry leaderboard rather than a preference-optimised language model, the objective is to dislodge one rank rather than to steer parameters towards a target, and an evaluator refits the exact model for verification. We cite this literature for positioning only and do not compare against any poisoning method, and we have not surveyed attacks on rank aggregation exhaustively.\n\n\\paragraph{Integrity of crowd-sourced leaderboards.}\n\\citet{Chiang2024Chatbot} describe the Chatbot Arena platform, in which users vote between two anonymous model responses and the votes are aggregated into rankings. \\citet{Min2025Rigging} study strategies by which votes on such platforms can be manipulated to change rankings, and \\citet{Singh2025Illusion} analyse evaluation practices on the same platform, including private testing and differences in data access and sampling across providers. \\citet{Zheng2023Judging} release expert human and GPT-4 pairwise judgments on MT-Bench. We use the Arena and MT-Bench comparison data, but our attack is a generic, evaluator-verified probe; we do not model any platform's actual voting interface or an adversary's access to it.\n\n\\section{Problem Setting and Method}\n\\item For every row compute $p_i$, $\\tilde y_i$ and $s_i=v_{a_i}-v_{b_i}$, and the effect $\\Delta_i$ of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects} for the requested mode; set $\\Delta_i=+\\infty$ for tie rows in flip mode.\n\\item Sort rows by $(\\Delta_i,\\,i)$, keep the first $k$, and drop those with $\\Delta_i\\ge0$ or non-finite $\\Delta_i$. Return the indices.\n\\end{enumerate}\nThe frozen configuration is identical to the starter code of the task (same source tree hash), so the studied method and the evaluator's influence baseline use the same first-order formulas, although the implementations differ in the details noted in the main text. % @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#_propose]]\nIts seed state is reset on request but is never used to make choices.\n\n\\subsection{Selection Context and Unscored Search Variants}\n\\label{app:selection}\nThe configuration studied here was chosen on a separate search data collection, on which it obtained a search-scope score of $0.914$ with a score delta of $0.0$ relative to the baseline (which is the selected configuration); the study outcome is recorded as completed without a qualifying winner and the selection kind is the baseline. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.baseline_score]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.investigation_outcome]]\nThree search variants were proposed as potential improvements: a calibrated minimal-perturbation sensitivity analysis across designs, a pair-class aggregated multi-rival greedy search with exact-refit selection, and rival-specific chunked re-linearised edit plans with an exact-refit prefix line search. All three produced no score ($3$ total, $0$ scored, $3$ unscored). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]\nUnscored variants are missing performance evidence, not measured losses, and no comparison involving them appears anywhere in this paper. The search-scope score $0.914$ and the final score $0.606$ come from different data collections and scopes, with different designs (the search collection has no expert-human or LLM-judge data); they must not be read as a decline or a generalisation gap.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\n\\label{app:llm}\nLarge language models were used throughout this work. An LLM-based research agent designed the three alternative search variants described above and wrote their code, none of which was scored; the studied search is the task's starter code. An LLM agent retrieved references through a literature-search API, planned the paper, wrote this manuscript and its claim-to-evidence mapping from the recorded evaluation artifacts, and produced the figures. The evaluation protocol, data, evaluator and scoring were fixed by the task specification before the run, and every number in the paper is read from the evaluator's recorded outputs. The starter references named in the task were taken from the task description.\n\n\\end{document}\n0 Final evaluation qualified and completed; qualification is an integrity gate, not superiority. ['final.qualification.outcome', 'fina