{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVPD1GTtNPsqQVQA1eUA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018gwcixfSYQhHVKxpVjdKZD","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'E'\nimport re\ns=open('paper.tex').read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(a)==1,a[:60]\n    s=s.replace(a,b)\nrep(r\"Second, crowd-sourced leaderboards can be influenced deliberately, for instance through vote rigging \\citep{Min2025Rigging} or through differences in data access and sampling between providers \\citep{Singh2025Illusion}.\",\nr\"Second, crowd-sourced leaderboards can be influenced deliberately: work on vote rigging on Chatbot Arena \\citep{Min2025Rigging} and on the reliability of leaderboard rankings \\citep{Singh2025Illusion} is motivation here (we know these two works from their titles only).\")\nrep(r\"approximate-robustness analysis of ``dropping a little data'' \\citep{Broderick2020Automatic}.\",\nr\"work on whether ``dropping a little data'' can change a conclusion \\citep{Broderick2020Automatic} (known to us from its title only).\")\nrep(r\"Extensions handle ties \\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, order effects \\citep{Davidson1977OnET}, covariates \\citep{Yan2025InferenceIA}, temporal drift \\citep{Tian2023ASA}, continuous outcomes \\citep{Stern2011ModeratedPC,Fageot2023GeneralizedBM} and Bayesian priors \\citep{Leonard1977AnAB}.\",\nr\"Related work, which we know from titles only, includes BT models with ties \\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, within-pair order effects \\citep{Davidson1977OnET}, covariates \\citep{Yan2025InferenceIA}, a dynamic BT model \\citep{Tian2023ASA}, continuous data \\citep{Stern2011ModeratedPC,Fageot2023GeneralizedBM} and a Bayesian approach \\citep{Leonard1977AnAB}.\")\nrep(r\"The model has also been used to derive priorities from comparison matrices \\citep{Genest1999DerivingPF} and has been related to PageRank \\citep{Selby2024PageRankAT}. Maximum likelihood estimation requires conditions on the comparison graph \\citep{Butler2004TheEO,Bong2021GeneralizedRF}, which motivates penalised fits \\citep{Varin2024TractableRR}; our evaluator uses a ridge penalty and counts a tie as half a win for each side, a convention in the spirit of tie-extended models. Algorithms for fitting generalised BT models have been analysed by \\citet{Vojnovic2019ConvergenceRO}, and the large-sample behaviour of the estimator when the number of competitors grows by \\citet{Simons1999AsymptoticsWT} and \\citet{Li2021ThePO}.\",\nr\"Further titled works concern priorities from the BT model \\citep{Genest1999DerivingPF}, its relation to PageRank \\citep{Selby2024PageRankAT}, existence of maximum likelihood estimates \\citep{Butler2004TheEO,Bong2021GeneralizedRF}, ridge regression for paired comparisons \\citep{Varin2024TractableRR}, convergence rates of fitting algorithms \\citep{Vojnovic2019ConvergenceRO}, and asymptotics of the BT model \\citep{Simons1999AsymptoticsWT,Li2021ThePO}. We do not rely on any result from these works; our evaluator uses a ridge penalty and counts a tie as half a win for each side.\")\nrep(r\"\\citet{Broderick2020Automatic} propose a finite-sample robustness metric asking whether dropping a small fraction of the data can change a conclusion, and use first-order influence approximations to find the data to drop. Our search is of this family, specialised to the top-versus-runner-up score gap of a BT fit and extended to outcome reversal. The linear approximation behind such analyses is the infinitesimal jackknife, which \\citet{Giordano2018Swiss} develop as a general approximation to refits with data removed; its accuracy is a property of the problem and of the size of the perturbation, and is not guaranteed for the 2\\% edits studied here. As in that literature, a first-order search finds candidate edit sets only; we therefore let the evaluator verify every edit by an exact refit.\",\nr\"\\citet{Broderick2020Automatic}, known to us from the title ``When Can Dropping a Little Data Make a Big Difference?'' only, ask whether dropping a small fraction of the data can change a conclusion. Our search shares the idea of choosing rows to drop by a first-order score, specialised to the top-versus-runner-up score gap of a BT fit and extended to outcome reversal, but we do not claim to reproduce their method. Linear approximations of this kind are related to the infinitesimal jackknife (\\citealp{Giordano2018Swiss}, known to us from the title only); we rely on no accuracy guarantee, and the approximation may be inaccurate for the 2\\% edits studied here. Because a first-order search finds candidate edit sets only, the evaluator verifies every edit by an exact refit.\")\nrep(r\"\\citet{Min2025Rigging} study strategies by which votes on such platforms can be manipulated to change rankings, and \\citet{Singh2025Illusion} analyse evaluation practices on the same platform, including private testing and differences in data access and sampling across providers.\",\nr\"\\citet{Min2025Rigging} (``Improving Your Model Ranking on Chatbot Arena by Vote Rigging'') and \\citet{Singh2025Illusion} (``The Leaderboard Illusion'') concern the integrity of such leaderboards; we know both from their titles only and do not describe their methods or findings.\")\nrep(r\"the accuracy of the underlying linear approximation is problem dependent \\citep{Giordano2018Swiss}, and we did not evaluate it for these data.\",\nr\"we did not evaluate the accuracy of the underlying linear approximation for these data.\")\nrep(r\"(different Arena partition and NBA seasons, and no expert-human or LLM-judge data)\",\nr\"(different Arena partition and NBA seasons, no expert-human or LLM-judge data, and an additional declared-synthetic planted near-tie family with weight 0.2 in the search-scope score)\")\nrep(r\"(the search collection has no expert-human or LLM-judge data); they must\",\nr\"(the search collection has no expert-human or LLM-judge data, and the search-scope score includes a declared-synthetic planted near-tie family with weight $0.2$); they must\")\nrep(r\"designed the three alternative search variants described above and wrote their code, none of which was scored;\",\nr\"proposed the three alternative search variants described above, none of which produced a score;\")\nopen('paper.tex','w').write(s)\nE\ngrep -n \"singh\\|Seetharaman2021\\|We know these\" paper.tex | head -3 | cut -c1-100\npython3 - <<'E'\nimport json\nc=json.load(open('claims_grounding.json'))\nc['claims'][11]['claim']=\"Background: titled related work on BT-model variants and estimation (ties, order effects, covariates, dynamic, Bayesian, ridge, existence of MLE, algorithms, asymptotics); attributed from titles only, no result relied upon.\"\nc['claims'][11]['source_ids']=['ref.starter.Bradley1952Rank']\nc['claims'][13]['claim']=\"Related work on influence functions, dropping-data robustness (title only), vote rigging and leaderboard illusion (titles only), Arena and MT-Bench data.\"\nc['claims'][9]['claim']=c['claims'][9]['claim']+\" Search-scope score includes a declared-synthetic planted near-tie family (weight 0.2) and is not comparable to the final score.\"\njson.dump(c,open('claims_grounding.json','w'),ensure_ascii=False,indent=1)\nE\ngrep -n \"ref\\.\" paper_data.json | head -0; python3 - <<'E'\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('/workspace/paper/references_provenance.json'))\nprint([r['id'] for r in d['references']][:60])\nE"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":872,"cache_read_input_tokens":53003,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":872,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"84f4710a-b57d-47ca-82ad-cb